## Solution

**Recognize the integrand as a real part.** Observe that

$$
e^{\cos\phi}\,e^{i(\sin\phi-\phi)} = e^{-i\phi}\,e^{\cos\phi+i\sin\phi} = e^{-i\phi}\,e^{e^{i\phi}}.
$$

Taking real parts and using that cosine is even,

$$
\operatorname{Re}\!\left(e^{-i\phi}e^{e^{i\phi}}\right) = e^{\cos\phi}\cos(\sin\phi-\phi) = e^{\cos\phi}\cos(\phi-\sin\phi).
$$

Hence

$$
I = \int_0^{2\pi} e^{\cos\phi}\cos(\phi-\sin\phi)\,d\phi = \operatorname{Re}\int_0^{2\pi} e^{-i\phi}\,e^{e^{i\phi}}\,d\phi.
$$

**Contour integration.** Set $z = e^{i\phi}$, so $dz = iz\,d\phi$, i.e. $d\phi = \dfrac{dz}{iz}$, and $e^{-i\phi} = z^{-1}$. The unit circle $|z|=1$ is traversed once counterclockwise as $\phi$ runs from $0$ to $2\pi$. Therefore

$$
\int_0^{2\pi} e^{-i\phi}\,e^{e^{i\phi}}\,d\phi = \oint_{|z|=1} z^{-1}\,e^{z}\,\frac{dz}{iz} = \frac{1}{i}\oint_{|z|=1}\frac{e^{z}}{z^{2}}\,dz.
$$

**Residue computation.** The integrand $\dfrac{e^{z}}{z^{2}}$ has a pole of order $2$ at $z=0$. Expanding $e^{z}=1+z+\dfrac{z^{2}}{2}+\cdots$,

$$
\frac{e^{z}}{z^{2}} = \frac{1}{z^{2}}+\frac{1}{z}+\frac{1}{2}+\cdots,
$$

so the residue at $z=0$ is $\operatorname{Res}_{z=0}\dfrac{e^{z}}{z^{2}} = 1$.

By the residue theorem,

$$
\frac{1}{i}\oint_{|z|=1}\frac{e^{z}}{z^{2}}\,dz = \frac{1}{i}\cdot 2\pi i\cdot 1 = 2\pi.
$$

**Conclusion.** The contour integral is purely real ($2\pi$), so taking the real part changes nothing:

$$
\boxed{\int_0^{2\pi} e^{\cos\phi}\cos(\phi-\sin\phi)\,d\phi = 2\pi.}
$$

(Consistency check: the imaginary part $\int_0^{2\pi}e^{\cos\phi}\sin(\sin\phi-\phi)\,d\phi$ vanishes by the substitution $\phi\mapsto 2\pi-\phi$, confirming the result is purely real.)

